counterfactuals.output:
The study gives a rigorous version of Wick rotation for quantum fields on static curved spaces. Starting with a Euclidean quantum field theory on a static Riemannian manifold equipped with a time-reflection symmetry, it assumes reflection positivity and invariance under the manifold’s Euclidean isometries.

Using Osterwalder–Schrader quantization, the authors:

- build a physical Hilbert space from observables supported in the positive-time region;
- show that Euclidean time translations generate a positive self-adjoint Hamiltonian;
- transfer spatial isometries directly to unitary operators;
- analytically continue the remaining Euclidean symmetry generators, turning them into generators of Lorentzian boosts or time-like motions.

Under suitable regularity, domain, and integrability conditions, these infinitesimal generators exponentiate to a unitary representation of the connected isometry group of the associated Lorentzian static spacetime. Thus, reflection positivity does more than reconstruct states and dynamics: it converts Euclidean geometric symmetry into a genuine unitary Lorentzian symmetry representation. The work extends the usual flat-space Euclidean-to-Minkowski reconstruction to static curved manifolds and clarifies the operator-theoretic role of Wick rotation.
